{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DD5FnmkMWUR5mVMU44bMfk","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Current Evaluation State\n     2→\n     3→## Active Text\n     4→- **Name:** frankenstein\n     5→- **Attempt:** 4 of 5\n     6→- **Phase:** awaiting_fix\n     7→\n     8→## Latest Scores\n     9→- Structure Detection: 10/10\n    10→- Character Extraction: 4/10 ← FAILING\n    11→- Character Profiles: 5/10 ← FAILING\n    12→- Chapter Summaries: 9/10\n    13→- Pronunciation Guide: 3/10 ← FAILING\n    14→- HTML Presentation: 9/10\n    15→- **Overall: 6.25/10** (threshold: 8.0)\n    16→\n    17→## Current Issues (Priority Order)\n    18→\n    19→### CRITICAL\n    20→1. **Character alias resolution failure: Alphonse Frankenstein massively fragmented**\n    21→   - Problem: \"my father\" (53 mentions), \"Father\" (6 mentions), \"M.\" (23 mentions), \"M. Frankenstein\" (2 mentions), and \"Alphonse Frankenstein\" (1 mention) are listed as FIVE separate characters\n    22→   - Evidence: All refer to Victor's father, Alphonse Frankenstein. They must be merged with \"Alphonse Frankenstein\" as canonical name.\n    23→   - Location: Character extraction and alias resolution in `src/agents/character_agent.py` or `src/pipeline/character_extraction/consensus.py`\n    24→   - **Analysis:** Attempt 3's validation fix did NOT work. The `_validate_merge` changes allow relational descriptors to pass validation, but they are STILL NOT MERGING. This suggests:\n    25→     1. The candidate pairs are not being generated for these combinations, OR\n    26→     2. The LLM pairwise merge decision is rejecting them, OR\n    27→     3. The merging logic after validation is failing\n    28→   - Need to add detailed logging to trace why \"my father\" + \"Alphonse Frankenstein\" are not merging despite validation changes\n    29→   - Impact: -2.0 points (core character badly fragmented into 5 pieces)\n    30→\n    31→2. **Missing character: William Frankenstein**\n    32→   - Problem: Victor's younger brother William (murder victim in Chapter 8) appears in chapter summaries (\"William Frankenstein\" is explicitly listed in Ch. 8 characters_present) but has NO character profile in the main character list\n    33→   - Evidence: Chapter 8 summary: \"After receiving a devastating letter from his father detailing the murder of his young brother William...\" and characters_present array includes \"William Frankenstein\"\n    34→   - Location: Character profile generation threshold in character agent, or character extraction missing him entirely despite chapter detection finding him\n    35→   - Fix: Check why characters appearing in `characters_present` are not making it to the final character list. May be filtered by mention count, but plot-critical characters should never be filtered.\n    36→   - Impact: -1.0 points (major plot character completely absent from character list)\n    37→\n    38→3. **Massive pronunciation false positives**\n    39→   - Problem: Common English words flagged unnecessarily: \"my\", \"man\", \"father\", \"old\", \"young\", \"child\", \"mother\", \"girl\", \"woman\", \"creature\", \"monster\", \"stranger\", \"creator\", \"magistrate\", \"Uncle\"\n    40→   - Evidence: 663 total pronunciations. First 30 entries include 15 common English words (50% false positive rate in sample). Words like \"my\", \"man\", \"father\", \"old\", \"young\", \"child\", \"mother\" are basic English vocabulary that no narrator needs pronunciation help with.\n    41→   - Location: `src/agents/pronunciation_agent.py` filtering logic\n    42→   - Fix: Implement word frequency filter using common English word lists (e.g., top 5000-10000 most common words). Exclude: articles, possessives, common nouns, common adjectives, common family terms. Only flag: proper nouns (names, places), foreign terms, technical/archaic terms, homographs with context.\n    43→   - Impact: -0.7 points (guide unusable due to noise)\n    44→\n    45→### HIGH\n    46→4. **Character fragmentation: Victor Frankenstein split 4 ways**\n    47→   - Problem: \"Victor\" (28 mentions), \"M. Frankenstein\" (2 mentions), \"Alphonse Frankenstein\" (1 mention), and \"Madame Frankenstein\" (1 mention) are all flagged as matching \"Victor Frankenstein\"\n    48→   - Evidence: \"Victor\" is the main character with 28 mentions. \"M. Frankenstein\" likely refers to both Victor and his father (title + surname). \"Alphonse\" and \"Madame\" are clearly Victor's parents, not Victor himself.\n    49→   - Location: Same core issue as Critical #1 - title/surname handling and family name disambiguation\n    50→   - Fix: Need to distinguish \"M. Frankenstein\" used for Victor vs his father based on context. \"Alphonse Frankenstein\" and \"Madame Frankenstein\" should never merge with \"Victor\"\n    51→   - Impact: -0.5 points\n    52→\n    53→5. **Character fragmentation: Henry Clerval split 3 ways**\n    54→   - Problem: \"Clerval\" (55 mentions), \"M. Clerval\" (2 mentions), and \"M.\" (23 mentions) as separate characters\n    55→   - Evidence: All refer to Henry Clerval, Victor's best friend\n    56→   - Location: Title handling in alias resolution + standalone \"M.\" extraction\n    57→   - Fix: Merge \"M. [Surname]\" with \"[Surname]\". The standalone \"M.\" should be filtered out or merged based on context.\n    58→   - Impact: -0.3 points\n    59→\n    60→6. **Character fragmentation: Robert Walton split 2 ways**\n    61→   - Problem: \"Walton\" (6 mentions) and \"Captain Walton\" (1 mention) are separate characters\n    62→   - Evidence: Both refer to Robert Walton, the Arctic explorer and frame narrator\n    63→   - Location: Title detection in alias resolution\n    64→   - Fix: Merge \"Title Name\" with \"Name\" (Captain, Mr., Mrs., Madame, M., Dr., etc.)\n    65→   - Impact: -0.15 points\n    66→\n    67→7. **Character fragmentation: Caroline Frankenstein split 2 ways**\n    68→   - Problem: \"my mother\" (10 mentions) and \"Madame Frankenstein\" (1 mention) are separate characters\n    69→   - Evidence: Both refer to Caroline Beaufort Frankenstein, Victor's mother\n    70→   - Location: Same root cause as Critical #1 - relational descriptors not merging with proper names\n    71→   - Fix: Same approach as Critical #1\n    72→   - Impact: -0.2 points\n    73→\n    74→8. **Nonsense character entry: \"M.\"**\n    75→   - Problem: \"M.\" listed as standalone character with 23 mentions and no useful description\n    76→   - Evidence: This is an incomplete name extraction - likely fragments from \"M. Waldman\", \"M. Krempe\", \"M. Frankenstein\", \"M. Clerval\"\n    77→   - Location: Name extraction validation in character extraction\n    78→   - Fix: Filter out standalone titles/honorifics that aren't followed by a name. \"M.\" alone should never be a character. Need post-extraction cleanup to merge or remove these.\n    79→   - Impact: -0.3 points (included in High #5 scoring)\n    80→\n    81→9. **Character fragmentation: Justine Moritz split 2 ways**\n    82→   - Problem: \"Justine\" (52 mentions) and \"Madame Moritz\" (2 mentions) as separate characters\n    83→   - Evidence: \"Justine\" is Justine Moritz, the servant falsely accused. \"Madame Moritz\" is her mother (different person). However, if analysis merged them, that's incorrect.\n    84→   - **NOTE:** Need to verify - these SHOULD be separate people. Justine Moritz is the daughter, Madame Moritz is the mother. If they're listed separately, that's CORRECT. Removing this as an issue unless verification shows they were incorrectly merged.\n    85→   - Impact: NONE (may be correct as-is)\n    86→\n    87→### MEDIUM\n    88→10. **De Lacey fragmentation**\n    89→    - Problem: \"De Lacey\" (9 mentions), \"De\" (6 mentions), and \"old man\" (2 mentions) potentially separate\n    90→    - Evidence: \"De Lacey\" is the blind father in the cottage. \"De\" is likely an incomplete extraction. \"old man\" may refer to De Lacey.\n    91→    - Location: Name extraction creating incomplete \"De\" + descriptor resolution for \"old man\"\n    92→    - Fix: Improve compound name handling for \"De Lacey\", \"Van Helsing\" style names. Merge descriptive references.\n    93→    - Impact: -0.1 points\n    94→\n    95→11. **Inconsistent canonical names using relational descriptors**\n    96→    - Problem: \"my father\" and \"my mother\" used as canonical names instead of \"Alphonse Frankenstein\" and \"Caroline Frankenstein\"\n    97→    - Evidence: Character profiles show \"my father\" (53 mentions) and \"my mother\" (10 mentions) as main headings rather than proper names\n    98→    - Location: Canonical name selection in consensus.py\n    99→    - Fix: Prefer proper names over relational descriptors when selecting canonical name. The proper name should be canonical, with relational terms as aliases.\n   100→    - Impact: Usability issue (scoring included in Critical #1 and High #7)\n   101→\n   102→### LOW\n   103→None at this time.\n   104→\n   105→## Fix History\n   106→### Attempt 1: Fixed CLI output path handling\n   107→**Issue:** CLI ignored explicit `--html` and `--output` flags, creating timestamped directory instead\n   108→\n   109→**Fix Applied:**\n   110→- Modified `src/cli.py` lines 367-368: Added `output_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)` for JSON output\n   111→- Modified `src/cli.py` lines 397-398: Added `html_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)` for HTML output\n   112→\n   113→**Testing:** All 444 tests pass.\n   114→\n   115→**Result:** PASSED - CLI now respects explicit output paths\n   116→\n   117→### Attempt 2: Enhanced alias candidate pair generation for relational descriptors\n   118→**Issue:** Critical #1 from Attempt 1 - Relational/familial descriptors like \"my father\", \"Father\" not being paired with proper names like \"Alphonse Frankenstein\" for alias resolution\n   119→\n   120→**Root Cause:** The `_candidate_pairs_for_merge` function in `src/pipeline/character_extraction/consensus.py` only generated candidate pairs based on:\n   121→1. Token overlap (shared words)\n   122→2. Substring matching\n   123→3. Known nickname variants\n   124→\n   125→Relational descriptors like \"my father\" share no tokens with \"Alphonse Frankenstein\", so they were never considered as candidates for the LLM pairwise merge decision.\n   126→\n   127→**Fix Applied:**\n   128→- Modified `src/pipeline/character_extraction/consensus.py` lines 934-987\n   129→- Added new section in `_candidate_pairs_for_merge` to identify relational/familial descriptors (father, mother, uncle, etc.) and descriptive patterns (old man, creature, stranger, etc.)\n   130→- Pairs each relational/descriptive name with top 30 proper names for LLM evaluation\n   131→- The LLM's pairwise merge logic decides if they actually refer to the same person based on context\n   132→\n   133→**Testing:** All 444 tests pass.\n   134→\n   135→**Result:** PARTIAL SUCCESS\n   136→- ✅ \"the creature\" successfully merged with \"the monster\" and \"the wretch\" (aliases: \"the monster, the wretch\")\n   137→- ❌ \"my father\" (53), \"Father\" (6), \"M.\" (23), \"M. Frankenstein\" (2), \"Alphonse Frankenstein\" (1) still separate\n   138→- ❌ \"Walton\" (6) and \"Captain Walton\" (1) still separate\n   139→- ❌ \"Clerval\" (55) and \"M. Clerval\" (2) still separate\n   140→\n   141→**Analysis:** The candidate pair generation is now working (proven by \"the creature\" merging correctly), but the LLM pairwise merge decision is rejecting merges for \"my father\" + \"Alphonse Frankenstein\" OR the validation step is blocking them.\n   142→\n   143→### Attempt 3: Fixed validation logic to accept relational descriptor merges\n   144→**Issue:** Critical #1 from Attempt 2 - The `_validate_merge` function was rejecting all merges with no shared words, including valid relational descriptor merges like \"my father\" + \"Alphonse Frankenstein\"\n   145→\n   146→**Root Cause Investigation:**\n   147→- Traced through `_validate_merge` function in `src/pipeline/character_extraction/consensus.py`\n   148→- Found that after checking various special cases, line 2001-2011 had a blanket rejection for ANY pair with zero shared words\n   149→- \"my father\" (words: \"my\", \"father\") and \"Alphonse Frankenstein\" (words: \"alphonse\", \"frankenstein\") share NO words\n   150→- Since both are multi-word names, they didn't match the single-word special case logic\n   151→- The code reached the final \"no shared words = reject\" clause and was blocked\n   152→\n   153→**Fix Applied:**\n   154→- Modified `src/pipeline/character_extraction/consensus.py` lines 2001-2042\n   155→- Added special case handling BEFORE the blanket rejection for relational/descriptive terms\n   156→- Detects if either name contains relational terms (father, mother, uncle, etc.) or descriptive patterns (creature, monster, man, etc.)\n   157→- For relational/descriptive pairs with chapter overlap, allow merge with confidence based on overlap ratio:\n   158→  - overlap_ratio > 0.3: confidence 0.75\n   159→  - any overlap: confidence 0.65\n   160→  - no overlap: reject with confidence 0.3\n   161→- This allows \"my father\" + \"Alphonse Frankenstein\" to merge if they appear in overlapping chapters\n   162→\n   163→**Testing:** All 444 tests pass.\n   164→\n   165→**Expected Impact:** Should significantly improve character alias resolution for Frankenstein, reducing character fragmentation.\n   166→\n   167→**Result:** FAILED\n   168→- ✅ \"the creature\" still correctly merged with \"the monster\" and \"the wretch\" (maintained from Attempt 2)\n   169→- ❌ \"my father\" (53), \"Father\" (6), \"M.\" (23), \"M. Frankenstein\" (2), \"Alphonse Frankenstein\" (1) STILL separate\n   170→- ❌ \"Walton\" (6) and \"Captain Walton\" (1) STILL separate\n   171→- ❌ \"Clerval\" (55) and \"M. Clerval\" (2) STILL separate\n   172→- ❌ \"my mother\" (10) and \"Madame Frankenstein\" (1) STILL separate\n   173→\n   174