{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A7STrh22uChTbXxqbRqNJc","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→{\n     2→  \"metadata\": {\n     3→    \"title\": \"Contents\",\n     4→    \"author\": \"Mary Wollstonecraft (Godwin) Shelley\",\n     5→    \"source_file\": \"Test_Texts/Frankenstein_ebook.txt\",\n     6→    \"source_format\": \"txt\",\n     7→    \"total_word_count\": 75060,\n     8→    \"total_character_count\": 418299,\n     9→    \"estimated_total_duration_minutes\": 499.2266666666667,\n    10→    \"words_per_minute\": 150\n    11→  },\n    12→  \"structure\": [\n    13→    {\n    14→      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n    15→      \"title\": null,\n    16→      \"index\": 1,\n    17→      \"start_position\": 1000,\n    18→      \"end_position\": 31434,\n    19→      \"word_count\": 5441,\n    20→      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 36.27333333333333,\n    21→      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n    22→      \"children\": [],\n    23→      \"characters_present\": [\n    24→        \"Robert Walton\",\n    25→        \"the lieutenant\",\n    26→        \"the master\",\n    27→        \"the stranger\"\n    28→      ],\n    29→      \"summary\": \"The chapter opens with Robert Walton preparing for an Arctic expedition in Archangel, Russia, having hired a ship and assembled a crew including a courageous but uneducated lieutenant and a gentle, respected master; he reflects on his lonely life, his abandoned poetic dreams, and his six-year dedication to physical training with whale-fishers and academic study in navigation and science, all fueled by an inherited fortune and a romantic obsession with polar discovery. His ship later becomes trapped in thick ice near the North Pole, where the crew witnesses a gigantic, man-like figure driving a dog-drawn sledge across the frozen expanse, followed days later by the discovery of a second sledge drifting on a fragment of ice, carrying a nearly frozen European stranger who is brought aboard and nursed back to life. The stranger, emaciated and haunted, reveals he was pursuing the same being seen on the ice, and upon hearing Walton express his own obsessive ambition for polar discovery, he collapses emotionally, pleading with Walton to abandon his quest, while revealing his profound grief over lost friendship and his belief that the pursuit of knowledge has led to his ruin; Walton, deeply moved by the stranger’s transcendent connection to nature, piercing insight, and soul-subduing voice, resolves to meticulously record the stranger’s impending narrative, which he will begin the next day.\"\n    30→    },\n    31→    {\n    32→      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n    33→      \"title\": null,\n    34→      \"index\": 2,\n    35→      \"start_position\": 31434,\n    36→      \"end_position\": 41575,\n    37→      \"word_count\": 1766,\n    38→      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 11.773333333333333,\n    39→      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    40→      \"children\": [],\n    41→      \"characters_present\": [\n    42→        \"Narrator (Victor)\",\n    43→        \"Father\",\n    44→        \"Mother (Caroline Beaufort)\",\n    45→        \"Beaufort\",\n    46→        \"Elizabeth Lavenza\",\n    47→        \"Peasant woman\",\n    48→        \"Peasant man\"\n    49→      ],\n    50→      \"summary\": \"The chapter recounts the noble lineage of the narrator’s family in Geneva, detailing how his father, a respected public servant, befriended the fallen merchant Beaufort and tirelessly sought him after his retreat into obscurity in Lucerne. Upon finding Beaufort dying in poverty, the father witnessed the extraordinary devotion of Beaufort’s daughter, Caroline, who survived through manual labor until her father’s death, after which the father took her under his protection. Two years later, he married Caroline, and their deep, reverential bond led them to travel through Italy and France, where the narrator was born in Naples and raised as their cherished only child. During a visit to the Lake of Como, they encountered a destitute peasant family nurturing a fair-haired orphan girl, Elizabeth Lavenza, whose ethereal beauty and noble bearing moved the narrator’s mother to adopt her, bringing her home as the narrator’s 'more than sister.' The narrator, then five years old, viewed Elizabeth as a divine gift promised to him, forming an inseparable bond that defined his childhood.\"\n    51→    },\n    52→    {\n    53→      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n    54→      \"title\": null,\n    55→      \"index\": 3,\n    56→      \"start_position\": 41575,\n    57→      \"end_position\": 54263,\n    58→      \"word_count\": 2206,\n    59→      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 14.706666666666667,\n    60→      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    61→      \"children\": [],\n    62→      \"characters_present\": [\n    63→        \"Victor Frankenstein (narrator)\",\n    64→        \"Elizabeth Lavenza\",\n    65→        \"Henry Clerval\",\n    66→        \"Victor's father\",\n    67→        \"A natural philosopher (unnamed)\"\n    68→      ],\n    69→      \"summary\": \"The chapter reflects on the narrator's idyllic childhood in Geneva and Belrive, where he grew up alongside Elizabeth, whose gentle spirit softened his intense nature, and Henry Clerval, whose romantic imagination complemented his own intellectual fervor. At thirteen, he discovers Cornelius Agrippa’s works during a storm-bound stay at an inn near Thonon, becoming obsessed with alchemy and the secrets of nature, despite his father’s dismissive remark. His fascination deepens with Paracelsus and Albertus Magnus, until a violent thunderstorm at age fifteen destroys an oak tree near their home, prompting a natural philosopher to explain electricity and galvanism—ideas that render his previous studies obsolete and lead him to abandon alchemy for mathematics. Though he experiences temporary peace and clarity, the narrator senses this shift as a futile attempt by fate to avert his inevitable destruction, as his thirst for forbidden knowledge remains unquenched.\"\n    70→    },\n    71→    {\n    72→      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n    73→      \"title\": null,\n    74→      \"index\": 4,\n    75→      \"start_position\": 54263,\n    76→      \"end_position\": 69512,\n    77→      \"word_count\": 2675,\n    78→      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 17.833333333333332,\n    79→      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n    80→      \"children\": [],\n    81→      \"characters_present\": [\n    82→        \"Protagonist\",\n    83→        \"Elizabeth\",\n    84→        \"Father\",\n    85→        \"Henry Clerval\",\n    86→        \"M. Krempe\",\n    87→        \"M. Waldman\"\n    88→      ],\n    89→      \"summary\": \"The chapter begins with the protagonist, still grieving his mother’s recent death, preparing to depart Geneva for the University of Ingolstadt by carriage, accompanied by his father, friend Henry Clerval, and Elizabeth, who has recovered from scarlet fever. Upon arrival, he settles into a solitary apartment and meets Professor Krempe, who harshly dismisses his childhood studies of alchemy as obsolete, deepening his disillusionment with modern science. Later, he attends a chemistry lecture by Professor Waldman, whose passionate praise of modern chemistry’s achievements—its exploration of nature’s deepest mysteries—ignites a profound transformation in the protagonist, who resolves to pursue scientific mastery with obsessive determination, ultimately deciding to abandon alchemy and dedicate himself to chemistry and natural philosophy under Waldman’s mentorship.\"\n    90→    },\n    91→    {\n    92→      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n    93→      \"title\": null,\n    94→      \"index\": 5,\n    95→      \"start_position\": 69512,\n    96→      \"end_position\": 83926,\n    97→      \"word_count\": 2535,\n    98→      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 16.9,\n    99→      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n   100→      \"children\": [],\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1e7418e6-6838-442e-828f-4b60f0eaec24","uuid":"bface9bf-1cfd-48b4-a3df-383e3fac3547","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/zacharymandrews/Tools/audiobook_agent/output/frankenstein/analysis.json","content":"{\n  \"metadata\": {\n    \"title\": \"Contents\",\n    \"author\": \"Mary Wollstonecraft (Godwin) Shelley\",\n    \"source_file\": \"Test_Texts/Frankenstein_ebook.txt\",\n    \"source_format\": \"txt\",\n    \"total_word_count\": 75060,\n    \"total_character_count\": 418299,\n    \"estimated_total_duration_minutes\": 499.2266666666667,\n    \"words_per_minute\": 150\n  },\n  \"structure\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n      \"title\": null,\n      \"index\": 1,\n      \"start_position\": 1000,\n      \"end_position\": 31434,\n      \"word_count\": 5441,\n      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 36.27333333333333,\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"children\": [],\n      \"characters_present\": [\n        \"Robert Walton\",\n        \"the lieutenant\",\n        \"the master\",\n        \"the stranger\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"The chapter opens with Robert Walton preparing for an Arctic expedition in Archangel, Russia, having hired a ship and assembled a crew including a courageous but uneducated lieutenant and a gentle, respected master; he reflects on his lonely life, his abandoned poetic dreams, and his six-year dedication to physical training with whale-fishers and academic study in navigation and science, all fueled by an inherited fortune and a romantic obsession with polar discovery. His ship later becomes trapped in thick ice near the North Pole, where the crew witnesses a gigantic, man-like figure driving a dog-drawn sledge across the frozen expanse, followed days later by the discovery of a second sledge drifting on a fragment of ice, carrying a nearly frozen European stranger who is brought aboard and nursed back to life. The stranger, emaciated and haunted, reveals he was pursuing the same being seen on the ice, and upon hearing Walton express his own obsessive ambition for polar discovery, he collapses emotionally, pleading with Walton to abandon his quest, while revealing his profound grief over lost friendship and his belief that the pursuit of knowledge has led to his ruin; Walton, deeply moved by the stranger’s transcendent connection to nature, piercing insight, and soul-subduing voice, resolves to meticulously record the stranger’s impending narrative, which he will begin the next day.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n      \"title\": null,\n      \"index\": 2,\n      \"start_position\": 31434,\n      \"end_position\": 41575,\n      \"word_count\": 1766,\n      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 11.773333333333333,\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"children\": [],\n      \"characters_present\": [\n        \"Narrator (Victor)\",\n        \"Father\",\n        \"Mother (Caroline Beaufort)\",\n        \"Beaufort\",\n        \"Elizabeth Lavenza\",\n        \"Peasant woman\",\n        \"Peasant man\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"The chapter recounts the noble lineage of the narrator’s family in Geneva, detailing how his father, a respected public servant, befriended the fallen merchant Beaufort and tirelessly sought him after his retreat into obscurity in Lucerne. Upon finding Beaufort dying in poverty, the father witnessed the extraordinary devotion of Beaufort’s daughter, Caroline, who survived through manual labor until her father’s death, after which the father took her under his protection. Two years later, he married Caroline, and their deep, reverential bond led them to travel through Italy and France, where the narrator was born in Naples and raised as their cherished only child. During a visit to the Lake of Como, they encountered a destitute peasant family nurturing a fair-haired orphan girl, Elizabeth Lavenza, whose ethereal beauty and noble bearing moved the narrator’s mother to adopt her, bringing her home as the narrator’s 'more than sister.' The narrator, then five years old, viewed Elizabeth as a divine gift promised to him, forming an inseparable bond that defined his childhood.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n      \"title\": null,\n      \"index\": 3,\n      \"start_position\": 41575,\n      \"end_position\": 54263,\n      \"word_count\": 2206,\n      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 14.706666666666667,\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"children\": [],\n      \"characters_present\": [\n        \"Victor Frankenstein (narrator)\",\n        \"Elizabeth Lavenza\",\n        \"Henry Clerval\",\n        \"Victor's father\",\n        \"A natural philosopher (unnamed)\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"The chapter reflects on the narrator's idyllic childhood in Geneva and Belrive, where he grew up alongside Elizabeth, whose gentle spirit softened his intense nature, and Henry Clerval, whose romantic imagination complemented his own intellectual fervor. At thirteen, he discovers Cornelius Agrippa’s works during a storm-bound stay at an inn near Thonon, becoming obsessed with alchemy and the secrets of nature, despite his father’s dismissive remark. His fascination deepens with Paracelsus and Albertus Magnus, until a violent thunderstorm at age fifteen destroys an oak tree near their home, prompting a natural philosopher to explain electricity and galvanism—ideas that render his previous studies obsolete and lead him to abandon alchemy for mathematics. Though he experiences temporary peace and clarity, the narrator senses this shift as a futile attempt by fate to avert his inevitable destruction, as his thirst for forbidden knowledge remains unquenched.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n      \"title\": null,\n      \"index\": 4,\n      \"start_position\": 54263,\n      \"end_position\": 69512,\n      \"word_count\": 2675,\n      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 17.833333333333332,\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"children\": [],\n      \"characters_present\": [\n        \"Protagonist\",\n        \"Elizabeth\",\n        \"Father\",\n        \"Henry Clerval\",\n        \"M. Krempe\",\n        \"M. Waldman\"\n      ],\n      \"summary\": \"The chapter begins with the protagonist, still grieving his mother’s recent death, preparing to depart Geneva for the University of Ingolstadt by carriage, accompanied by his father, friend Henry Clerval, and Elizabeth, who has recovered from scarlet fever. Upon arrival, he settles into a solitary apartment and meets Professor Krempe, who harshly dismisses his childhood studies of alchemy as obsolete, deepening his disillusionment with modern science. Later, he attends a chemistry lecture by Professor Waldman, whose passionate praise of modern chemistry’s achievements—its exploration of nature’s deepest mysteries—ignites a profound transformation in the protagonist, who resolves to pursue scientific mastery with obsessive determination, ultimately deciding to abandon alchemy and dedicate himself to chemistry and natural philosophy under Waldman’s mentorship.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"chapter\",\n      \"title\": null,\n      \"index\": 5,\n      \"start_position\": 69512,\n      \"end_position\": 83926,\n      \"word_count\": 2535,\n      \"estimated_duration_minutes\": 16.9,\n      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n      \"children\": [],","numLines":100,"startLine":1,"totalLines":15657}}}
